::MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.5
Question:
Find simple interest on $300 at 5% for 2 years.
{
=A. $30#Correct. Use I \= Prt: 300 x 0.05 x 2 \= 30.
~B. $15#This uses only one year of interest.
~C. $300#This is the principal, not interest.
~D. $330#This is the final balance, not interest.
}
::MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.5
Question:
Simple interest is $45 on a $500 principal. What is the final balance?
{
~A. $455#This subtracts interest instead of adding it.
=B. $545#Correct. Add principal and interest: 500 + 45 \= 545.
~C. $45#This is only the interest.
~D. $500#This ignores the interest.
}
::MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.6
Question:
Which statement describes compound interest?
{
~A. Interest is calculated only on the original principal.#This describes simple interest. Compound interest uses the updated balance after interest is added.
~B. Interest only applies to taxes.#This confuses interest with taxes. Interest is money earned or charged on savings, loans, or investments.
=C. Interest is calculated on the updated balance after previous interest is added.#Correct. Compound interest uses the updated balance, so previous interest can also earn or be charged interest.
~D. Compound interest applies only to one-day loans.#This is too narrow. Compound interest can apply to savings, loans, and investments over many time periods.
}
::MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.6
Question:
Why can compound interest grow faster than simple interest?
{
~A. the rate is always 100%#The rate does not have to be 100%.
~B. the principal disappears#The principal remains part of the balance.
~C. time is ignored#Time is important in interest calculations.
=D. future interest can be earned on previous interest#Correct. Compound interest uses the updated balance, so interest can earn more interest.
}
::MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.5
Question:
A loan has principal $700 and final balance $770. What is the interest?
{
~A. $1,470#This adds the balance and principal.
=B. $70#Correct. Subtract principal from final balance: 770 - 700 \= 70.
~C. $770#This is the final balance, not interest.
~D. $700#This is the principal, not interest.
}
